WEBVTT

00:00:19.250 --> 00:00:22.080
Ever heard of a curse-bearer, Noé?

00:00:22.080 --> 00:00:23.210
Huh?

00:00:23.210 --> 00:00:30.130
That's what they call vampires who
suddenly lose their minds and go berserk.

00:00:30.130 --> 00:00:32.680
My grandfather told me about them.

00:00:32.680 --> 00:00:36.010
A curse? Whose?

00:00:36.010 --> 00:00:40.560
Who else? The Vampire of the Blue Moon's.

00:00:42.100 --> 00:00:43.730
Are you okay?

00:00:56.290 --> 00:00:59.750
Noé! Let's play!

00:00:59.750 --> 00:01:01.210
Noé.

00:01:01.210 --> 00:01:05.420
Even with a curse-bearer here before you,

00:01:05.420 --> 00:01:12.340
are you still able to see the
blue moon as something beautiful?

00:01:26.480 --> 00:01:32.530
THE 
CASE
 STUDY 
OF 
VANITAS

00:01:35.160 --> 00:01:40.200
<i>You're pondering the simple things,</i>
<i>pretty much: human emotions, and love?</i>

00:01:40.200 --> 00:01:44.580
<i>I hope one day you'll understand</i>

00:01:44.580 --> 00:01:49.170
<i>Too many wounds have left you</i>
<i>patched together, a miserable sight</i>

00:01:49.170 --> 00:01:54.430
<i>That doesn't even know</i>
<i>the definition of "alive"</i>

00:01:54.430 --> 00:02:01.930
<i>No need to say,</i>
<i>"Just the two of us" or anything</i>

00:02:01.930 --> 00:02:10.530
<i>I've a feeling we can understand</i>
<i>each other, and that's fine for now</i>

00:02:12.030 --> 00:02:19.490
<i>I love this world,</i>
<i>and the light only you give me</i>

00:02:19.490 --> 00:02:24.370
<i>Makes the world I see</i>
<i>through these fractured eyes </i>

00:02:24.370 --> 00:02:29.040
<i>Look gleaming and bright</i>

00:02:29.040 --> 00:02:33.630
<i>There's no such thing as a sure thing</i>

00:02:33.630 --> 00:02:38.430
<i>But you can see me, can't you?</i>

00:02:41.890 --> 00:02:47.230
<i>When sky and void meet, look back</i>
<i>because the dreams will come later</i>

00:02:52.740 --> 00:02:55.200
Vanitas.

00:02:55.200 --> 00:02:56.860
Yes?

00:02:56.860 --> 00:03:00.870
Can you explain why we're behind bars?

00:03:00.870 --> 00:03:03.000
A fine question, my dear Noé.

00:03:03.000 --> 00:03:08.130
About ten hours ago, you were poisoned by
the Eglantine due to your absurd antics.

00:03:08.130 --> 00:03:10.090
Then you face-planted by the roadside.

00:03:10.090 --> 00:03:13.920
You've slept like a baby ever since,
nearly half a day to be exact.

00:03:13.920 --> 00:03:16.430
That about sums it up.
You up to speed now?

00:03:16.430 --> 00:03:17.550
No, I'm not.

00:03:17.550 --> 00:03:18.840
Seriously?

00:03:18.840 --> 00:03:21.760
You were the one who
stole aboard the airship.

00:03:21.760 --> 00:03:25.060
I just happened to be there,
so how did I end up in prison, too?

00:03:25.060 --> 00:03:28.270
Because I told them
we're partners in crime,

00:03:28.270 --> 00:03:30.440
you moron!

00:03:30.440 --> 00:03:31.980
My wallet's missing!

00:03:31.980 --> 00:03:34.190
They confiscated all our belongings.

00:03:34.190 --> 00:03:36.280
What?! <i>The Book of Vanitas</i>, too?!

00:03:36.280 --> 00:03:37.240
Naturally.

00:03:37.240 --> 00:03:40.280
H-How can you remain so calm?!

00:03:40.280 --> 00:03:42.870
Wait, were you after my book?

00:03:42.870 --> 00:03:44.290
No!

00:03:44.290 --> 00:03:50.790
My master merely requested
that I assess its true nature.

00:03:50.790 --> 00:03:52.420
Assess, you say?

00:03:52.420 --> 00:03:56.800
That's quite a vague way
for your teacher to word it.

00:03:56.800 --> 00:04:01.550
That's true. He didn't
say steal or destroy it.

00:04:01.550 --> 00:04:05.430
What is it my teacher wants me to do?

00:04:05.430 --> 00:04:07.350
Wait a second, Noé!

00:04:07.350 --> 00:04:08.190
Yes?

00:04:08.190 --> 00:04:14.320
If you seek <i>The Book of Vanitas</i>,
then you should join me.

00:04:14.320 --> 00:04:17.990
So why do you keep refusing to help?

00:04:19.240 --> 00:04:21.620
That's true!

00:04:21.620 --> 00:04:23.530
Jeez.

00:04:30.710 --> 00:04:32.750
Now I understand, Vanitas!

00:04:32.750 --> 00:04:37.380
Perhaps I simply don't like you that much!

00:04:37.380 --> 00:04:38.880
What?

00:04:38.880 --> 00:04:40.470
What shall I do?

00:04:40.470 --> 00:04:43.890
For my teacher's sake,
I suppose I should go with you.

00:04:43.890 --> 00:04:46.720
But meekly accepting
whatever someone I dislike says

00:04:46.720 --> 00:04:49.100
is bad for my mental well-being,

00:04:49.100 --> 00:04:52.400
or rather,
it's psychologically impossible.

00:04:52.400 --> 00:04:55.650
Okay, so what now?

00:04:57.110 --> 00:04:58.070
Out.

00:04:58.070 --> 00:05:02.870
We're unable to hold you here any longer.

00:05:02.870 --> 00:05:06.030
This is an invitation from Count Orlok.

00:05:09.370 --> 00:05:13.250
The vampires likely came to
an arrangement with the humans.

00:05:13.250 --> 00:05:14.540
Huh?

00:05:14.540 --> 00:05:17.590
Come along. I'll show you the way.

00:05:17.590 --> 00:05:20.800
Thank you. This is my first time in Paris.

00:05:20.800 --> 00:05:22.340
Is that so?

00:05:43.320 --> 00:05:47.370
Welcome to Paris, the City of Flowers.

00:06:06.140 --> 00:06:09.890
The invitation from the Count
said to come get our things,

00:06:09.890 --> 00:06:15.650
both those the police took
and those we left on the airship.

00:06:17.610 --> 00:06:20.440
Why are you so exhausted, Vanitas?

00:06:20.440 --> 00:06:24.860
A certain someone keeps
wandering off when I'm not looking.

00:06:27.740 --> 00:06:31.370
We're here. Galerie Valentine.

00:06:31.370 --> 00:06:33.370
This is where we'll find the Count?

00:06:33.370 --> 00:06:36.710
That's right.
After the war with humankind,

00:06:36.710 --> 00:06:39.880
vampires were forbidden
from attacking humans.

00:06:39.880 --> 00:06:43.880
After that, most of them
vanished beyond the barrier.

00:06:43.880 --> 00:06:50.220
But there are still many who
continue to live among humankind.

00:06:50.220 --> 00:06:54.440
A vampire has been appointed by the
queen to maintain the balance between

00:06:54.440 --> 00:06:56.980
the vampires and humans here in Paris.

00:06:56.980 --> 00:06:59.270
He is...

00:06:59.270 --> 00:07:02.820
...the Lord of Altus, Parks Orlok.

00:07:04.190 --> 00:07:05.780
Don't move.

00:07:05.780 --> 00:07:09.780
Is this how you welcome invited guests?

00:07:09.780 --> 00:07:11.200
Silence.

00:07:11.200 --> 00:07:16.040
Ill-mannered fools who do not knock
are no better than trespassers.

00:07:20.920 --> 00:07:26.260
So you're the human who has
assumed the name of Vanitas.

00:07:30.100 --> 00:07:34.310
Murr! I'm so relieved you're safe!

00:07:34.310 --> 00:07:36.440
Your possessions.

00:07:36.440 --> 00:07:39.230
What about the rest?

00:07:43.650 --> 00:07:46.110
I'll get right to the point.

00:07:46.110 --> 00:07:50.200
Hand over <i>The Book of Vanitas</i>
and Amelia Ruth. I want them back.

00:07:50.200 --> 00:07:54.660
Vanitas, Mademoiselle Amelia
is fine now, so--

00:07:54.660 --> 00:07:58.000
Curse-bearers cannot be allowed to live.

00:07:58.000 --> 00:08:02.840
We will take it upon
ourselves to eliminate her.

00:08:02.840 --> 00:08:04.760
Please, Count, don't!

00:08:04.760 --> 00:08:07.300
Mademoiselle Amelia should be cured!

00:08:07.300 --> 00:08:10.550
You mean by using this book?

00:08:10.550 --> 00:08:11.720
That's right!

00:08:11.720 --> 00:08:14.010
I saw him use <i>The Book of Vanitas</i>.

00:08:14.010 --> 00:08:16.770
Its power restored her
to her former state.

00:08:16.770 --> 00:08:18.850
Therefore--

00:08:18.850 --> 00:08:20.730
Don't be absurd!

00:08:20.730 --> 00:08:24.480
Do you truly believe this fool's nonsense?

00:08:24.480 --> 00:08:27.650
There is no such thing
as a cursed grimoire.

00:08:27.650 --> 00:08:30.860
This thing is but rubbish.

00:08:33.120 --> 00:08:38.000
Or perhaps you stole it
but don't know how to use it.

00:08:38.000 --> 00:08:40.960
I expect you can't even open it.
That's why you summoned me.

00:08:40.960 --> 00:08:42.880
This is hilarious!

00:08:42.880 --> 00:08:48.090
Couldn't you just say,
"Please teach me how to use the book"?

00:08:48.090 --> 00:08:50.220
You are mistaken.

00:08:50.220 --> 00:08:53.800
I summoned you, the man who
assumed the name of Vanitas,

00:08:53.800 --> 00:08:57.470
to rake you over the coals
till you confess your plot.

00:08:57.470 --> 00:09:01.940
I'm simply trying to save vampire kind
from utter annihilation.

00:09:01.940 --> 00:09:03.650
Save us?!

00:09:03.650 --> 00:09:05.650
Pretentious, human!

00:09:05.650 --> 00:09:10.030
Are you so loathe to admit
<i>The Book of Vanitas </i>is real?!

00:09:10.030 --> 00:09:14.120
That Amelia regained her sanity
should be as plain as day.

00:09:14.120 --> 00:09:20.580
Bourreaus must behead
curse-bearers as soon as they are found.

00:09:22.080 --> 00:09:24.290
Mina is to be executed tomorrow?

00:09:24.290 --> 00:09:25.540
I don't understand!

00:09:25.540 --> 00:09:27.500
Because she's a curse-bearer.

00:09:27.500 --> 00:09:30.300
They're beheaded by a bourreau.
That's the rule.

00:09:30.300 --> 00:09:31.680
We must go save her!

00:09:31.680 --> 00:09:34.090
Can you lift the curse of the blue moon?

00:09:34.090 --> 00:09:35.680
Of course, you can't!

00:09:35.680 --> 00:09:39.100
She is beyond all help now, Noé!

00:09:39.100 --> 00:09:40.520
Hear me now, human!

00:09:40.520 --> 00:09:45.480
Vampires whose names have been defiled
can never return to their former state.

00:09:45.480 --> 00:09:50.440
This is what's wrong with you senile old
coots bound by antiquated traditions.

00:09:50.440 --> 00:09:51.740
You wretch!

00:09:51.740 --> 00:09:55.120
There is nothing you can do.

00:10:04.460 --> 00:10:06.540
How about this?

00:10:07.630 --> 00:10:11.010
The vampire attacks sowing
chaos throughout Paris

00:10:11.010 --> 00:10:14.680
are the work of a curse-bearer,
are they not?

00:10:14.680 --> 00:10:20.600
Let us go capture that vampire
and bring the fiend back here.

00:10:20.600 --> 00:10:25.230
Then Vanitas will use the
book's power here before you!

00:10:25.230 --> 00:10:30.360
You must see what <i>The Book of Vanitas</i>
is with your own eyes!

00:10:31.900 --> 00:10:36.410
Very well. We shall wait but one day.

00:10:37.450 --> 00:10:40.450
I thank you.

00:10:41.750 --> 00:10:44.250
We must hurry!

00:10:44.250 --> 00:10:47.040
At last, you wish to help me, Noé!

00:10:47.040 --> 00:10:49.550
I'm not helping you.

00:10:49.550 --> 00:10:52.550
It's <i>you </i>who is helping <i>me</i>!

00:11:02.810 --> 00:11:06.850
Excellent! I knew you'd
prove quite amusing!

00:11:08.190 --> 00:11:11.610
There's nothing you can do.

00:11:11.610 --> 00:11:16.410
Not this time.
I will not fail to save her!

00:11:19.120 --> 00:11:22.120
Noé. Hey, Noé, wait!

00:11:22.120 --> 00:11:25.540
Do you know where you're supposed to go?

00:11:25.540 --> 00:11:27.500
I've no idea--

00:11:27.500 --> 00:11:30.420
Looks like you could use a hand, Vanitas.

00:11:30.420 --> 00:11:31.500
Dante!

00:11:31.500 --> 00:11:32.380
Hey.

00:11:32.380 --> 00:11:36.680
You left Amelia Ruth behind
to save your own skin!

00:11:36.680 --> 00:11:41.470
My agreement with you didn't
include protecting the target!

00:11:41.470 --> 00:11:43.600
I don't work beyond what I'm paid!

00:11:43.600 --> 00:11:44.850
Greedy jerk!

00:11:44.850 --> 00:11:46.690
Shut your piehole, quack!

00:11:46.690 --> 00:11:48.520
Excuse me.

00:11:48.520 --> 00:11:51.070
What the hell are you doing with him?!

00:11:51.070 --> 00:11:53.610
Noé, allow me to introduce you.

00:11:53.610 --> 00:11:56.740
This is Dante,
an information peddler I often use.

00:11:56.740 --> 00:12:00.490
But he's really just someone
who'll do anything for money.

00:12:00.490 --> 00:12:02.910
Now I remember. The one you called baldy.

00:12:02.910 --> 00:12:05.910
I'm not bald!
Don't remember me by that slur!

00:12:05.910 --> 00:12:09.370
I don't know why you're here,
Dante, but it's your loss!

00:12:09.370 --> 00:12:12.420
I already found my penultimate partner!

00:12:12.420 --> 00:12:15.630
Penultimate partner? You mean this guy?

00:12:15.630 --> 00:12:16.670
Nope, not me.

00:12:16.670 --> 00:12:19.050
So, sorry, Dante.

00:12:19.050 --> 00:12:22.260
You'll have to look elsewhere
for your allowance.

00:12:22.260 --> 00:12:24.560
Really? That's too bad.

00:12:24.560 --> 00:12:28.390
Just when I located that killer
who claimed nine lives.

00:12:28.390 --> 00:12:30.310
What a crying shame.

00:12:30.310 --> 00:12:33.360
O Lord Dante, how I love thee!

00:12:35.280 --> 00:12:39.280
What? That's highway robbery, baldy!

00:12:39.280 --> 00:12:42.490
Shut it! Old man Orlok
doesn't even know about this.

00:12:42.490 --> 00:12:45.040
Such information doesn't come cheap!

00:12:46.450 --> 00:12:49.160
We should follow this bat?

00:12:49.160 --> 00:12:55.550
Exactly. It will lead you to my comrade
who is tailing the killer you seek.

00:13:03.300 --> 00:13:06.060
Seriously, what's that guy's deal?

00:13:06.060 --> 00:13:09.480
Was that <i>the </i>Vanitas?

00:13:09.480 --> 00:13:12.730
Such an adorable face, don't you think?

00:13:12.730 --> 00:13:15.020
You followed me here, Johann?

00:13:15.020 --> 00:13:20.570
I have something very special
I wanted to tell you myself.

00:13:20.570 --> 00:13:23.410
Lord Ruthven's bourreau is here in Paris.

00:13:23.410 --> 00:13:24.620
What?!

00:13:24.620 --> 00:13:27.080
He's after <i>The Book of Vanitas</i>, no doubt.

00:13:27.080 --> 00:13:30.750
And that friend of yours

00:13:30.750 --> 00:13:33.380
may end up dead.

00:13:37.000 --> 00:13:38.590
Thomas Berneux.

00:13:38.590 --> 00:13:39.550
What?

00:13:39.550 --> 00:13:43.590
He's the vampire who came from
beyond the barrier two months ago

00:13:43.590 --> 00:13:47.560
and killed twelve by draining their blood.

00:13:47.560 --> 00:13:53.150
I'd been pursuing him up until
I received a letter from Amelia Ruth.

00:13:53.150 --> 00:13:55.230
So, I already know much about him.

00:13:55.230 --> 00:13:59.440
So then you're the doctor
Mademoiselle Amelia mentioned?

00:13:59.440 --> 00:14:01.110
<i>Oui.</i>

00:14:12.000 --> 00:14:13.790
There he is. I'm sure of it.

00:14:13.790 --> 00:14:15.380
Thomas Berneux.

00:14:15.380 --> 00:14:16.420
Stop him, Noé!

00:14:16.420 --> 00:14:19.050
Very well!

00:14:24.380 --> 00:14:26.930
Are you trying to kill me?!

00:15:01.960 --> 00:15:04.880
I paralyzed him for a short while.

00:15:04.880 --> 00:15:09.470
Let's tie him up while we can
and take him back to Orlok.

00:15:14.890 --> 00:15:16.560
That was amazing.

00:15:16.560 --> 00:15:21.110
You so swiftly incapacitated
a curse-bearer.

00:15:21.110 --> 00:15:24.110
You must be Vanitas.

00:15:24.110 --> 00:15:25.740
I am Luca.

00:15:25.740 --> 00:15:28.870
And this is Jeanne.

00:15:28.870 --> 00:15:31.160
Are you humans or vampires?

00:15:31.160 --> 00:15:33.620
How rude of me.

00:15:33.620 --> 00:15:36.330
We are vampires.

00:15:36.330 --> 00:15:37.880
Monsieur Vanitas.

00:15:37.880 --> 00:15:41.710
Though it may seem ill-mannered,
I have a favor to ask.

00:15:41.710 --> 00:15:47.800
Would you not give me that grimoire,
<i>The Book of Vanitas</i>?

00:15:49.470 --> 00:15:50.760
No way in hell!

00:15:50.760 --> 00:15:55.680
This book isn't some plaything
for little brats like you!

00:16:02.980 --> 00:16:05.360
Why do you want it, anyway?

00:16:05.360 --> 00:16:10.030
<i>The Book of Vanitas </i>can
corrupt a vampire's true name.

00:16:10.030 --> 00:16:13.620
That is why it is known as an analytical
engine in the form of a book.

00:16:13.620 --> 00:16:19.250
It is the infernal device that creates
those known as curse-bearers!

00:16:20.380 --> 00:16:26.220
Are you suggesting that I'm using
<i>The Book of Vanitas </i>to spread the curse?

00:16:26.220 --> 00:16:27.380
I am.

00:16:27.380 --> 00:16:33.010
I shall save the curse-bearers by
destroying that book in the proper manner.

00:16:33.010 --> 00:16:37.310
And as its bearer, I imagine
you'll be punished accordingly.

00:16:37.310 --> 00:16:39.480
I'm afraid I can't let that happen!

00:16:39.480 --> 00:16:40.310
Hurray!

00:16:40.310 --> 00:16:43.190
But do as you will with Vanitas himself.

00:16:43.190 --> 00:16:44.020
Boo!

00:16:44.020 --> 00:16:46.030
We must make haste.

00:16:46.030 --> 00:16:48.320
Forgive me. Let us discuss later--

00:16:48.320 --> 00:16:50.950
I, too, have no time!

00:16:50.950 --> 00:16:56.410
Someone dear to me suffers terribly
as a curse-bearer, and I must save them!

00:16:56.410 --> 00:16:57.910
Unbelievable.

00:16:57.910 --> 00:17:00.960
Who put that nonsense in your head?

00:17:00.960 --> 00:17:02.290
Huh?

00:17:02.290 --> 00:17:04.550
Listen. I'm a doctor.

00:17:04.550 --> 00:17:08.970
I'm using the power of
<i>The Book of Vanitas </i>to save vampires.

00:17:08.970 --> 00:17:13.600
If you wish to save the one you hold dear,
I will exam the patient.

00:17:13.600 --> 00:17:19.140
My big brother suffers dearly from
the vampire of the blue moon's curse!

00:17:19.140 --> 00:17:23.150
For you to suggest that I take you
to him is beyond all foolishness!

00:17:23.150 --> 00:17:24.770
Master Luca.

00:17:26.020 --> 00:17:30.150
Hmm. So your brother is the curse-bearer.

00:17:36.290 --> 00:17:40.540
Master Luca,
further talk serves no purpose.

00:17:58.220 --> 00:18:01.440
Master Luca, command me as you will.

00:18:01.440 --> 00:18:06.860
Monsieur Vanitas, will nothing
convince you to give me the book?

00:18:09.740 --> 00:18:11.320
Jeanne...

00:18:11.320 --> 00:18:15.950
Understood. I will do
my best to take him alive.

00:18:17.280 --> 00:18:19.450
Please.

00:18:19.450 --> 00:18:22.370
Take <i>The Book of Vanitas </i>from him!

00:18:22.370 --> 00:18:25.080
<i>Oui, Votre Altesse.</i>

00:18:25.080 --> 00:18:28.000
The Crimson Gauntlet!

00:18:31.010 --> 00:18:34.720
Run, Noé! She's Lord Ruthven's bourreau!

00:18:34.720 --> 00:18:36.140
Who?

00:18:36.140 --> 00:18:40.140
You're like a country bumpkin squared!

00:18:40.140 --> 00:18:41.100
Listen up!

00:18:41.100 --> 00:18:42.270
In the war long ago,

00:18:42.270 --> 00:18:46.060
over 1,000 vampires sided with humanity,
betraying their own kind.

00:18:46.060 --> 00:18:49.690
But a single bourreau is
said to have slain them all.

00:18:49.690 --> 00:18:53.030
It is she who wields the
Crimson Gauntlet, Carpe Diem!

00:18:53.030 --> 00:18:57.200
The bearer of a saintly name
who slew her own kind!

00:18:59.160 --> 00:19:02.540
The Hellfire Witch, Jeanne!

00:19:12.800 --> 00:19:16.140
She has no intention of taking me alive!

00:19:17.550 --> 00:19:20.600
I can't have you die on me yet!

00:19:27.560 --> 00:19:29.570
We're in trouble!

00:19:48.210 --> 00:19:49.540
Jeanne!

00:19:49.540 --> 00:19:51.340
Master Luca?! You're in danger!

00:19:51.340 --> 00:19:54.130
Please find a safe place to hide!

00:19:54.130 --> 00:19:56.880
Jeanne! You must not slay him!

00:19:56.880 --> 00:20:01.760
You're no longer a bourreau!
You're my knight, my chevalier!

00:20:06.520 --> 00:20:10.020
Forgive me, Master Luca.

00:20:20.450 --> 00:20:23.700
Whether it's Count Orlok
or the city's vampires,

00:20:23.700 --> 00:20:26.790
they're all horrible listeners.

00:20:26.790 --> 00:20:32.840
All vampires have the fear of the vampire
of the blue moon etched into their minds.

00:20:32.840 --> 00:20:34.420
That's just how it is.

00:20:34.420 --> 00:20:35.300
Huh?

00:20:35.300 --> 00:20:40.140
And what that kid said
is not entirely wrong.

00:20:40.140 --> 00:20:43.930
<i>The Book of Vanitas </i>can
corrupt a vampire's true name.

00:20:43.930 --> 00:20:45.930
That much is true.

00:20:45.930 --> 00:20:48.770
A corrupted name causes a malnomen.

00:20:48.770 --> 00:20:52.770
My treatment consists of restoring
that name to its true state.

00:20:52.770 --> 00:20:55.780
In short, it's a matter of application.

00:20:55.780 --> 00:21:00.410
So it's no wonder that
vampires fear this book.

00:21:00.410 --> 00:21:06.250
But the potential to do something
and doing it are two different things!

00:21:06.250 --> 00:21:11.580
Even if <i>The Book of Vanitas</i>
was created to exterminate vampires,

00:21:11.580 --> 00:21:16.590
it's an undeniable fact that you used it
to save Mademoiselle Amelia.

00:21:17.920 --> 00:21:19.840
Thank you!

00:21:19.840 --> 00:21:25.220
What I saw that day was
a resoundingly righteous act.

00:21:29.440 --> 00:21:34.480
That is why I wish to return
at once so we may save her!

00:21:39.990 --> 00:21:43.530
Keep it down before she finds us!

00:21:43.530 --> 00:21:45.700
You really are...

00:21:45.700 --> 00:21:49.790
...a most amusing fellow.

00:21:49.790 --> 00:21:52.000
What?

00:21:53.290 --> 00:21:57.250
Now then! I've steeled myself, Noé!

00:21:57.250 --> 00:22:01.800
I know not whether this will work,
but it's our only hope for a swift return.

00:22:01.800 --> 00:22:04.180
What are you--?

00:22:04.180 --> 00:22:06.760
I've found but one way.

00:22:06.760 --> 00:22:10.640
One way we can defeat the Hellfire Witch.

00:22:14.640 --> 00:22:17.060
In the City of Flowers

00:23:48.610 --> 00:23:52.070
Fangs That Lay Bare Blood
